  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "The Gauge Room",
    "overview": "A calm instrument room for a control system. Warm paper is the floor, petrol teal is the structure and the brand, and a single amber needle marks the one thing to do next. Nothing performs. Every number, status and code sits where an operator expects to find it, and the same screen has to read at a pump in noon sun, at a police desk, and on a supervisor's monitor.\n\nThe system is Arabic and right-to-left first, with one typeface family and soft white cards that float on the paper rather than boxes drawn with rules. Color is a vocabulary with strict meanings: teal is structure and brand, amber is the call to action, green is approved, red is a rule that was broken or an action that cannot be undone. Because a screen is often read in direct sun, color never carries meaning alone. Every status also has a word or an icon.\n\nThe Directorate's real emblem is not part of this system. The product has its own symbol and name, and nothing in the interface imitates an official or state emblem.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "Warm paper floor, white floating cards, petrol-teal structure.",
      "Amber is reserved for the single primary action on a screen.",
      "Red means a broken rule or an irreversible action, nothing else.",
      "Pill-shaped controls, 16px cards, 10px fields.",
      "Cairo for everything; JetBrains Mono only for literal codes and big numbers.",
      "Every status is a word or icon plus color, never color alone."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The Meaning-Fixed Rule",
        "body": "Each hue carries one meaning: teal is structure and brand, amber is the next action, green is approved, red is broken or irreversible. Never borrow a hue for decoration.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The One Needle Rule",
        "body": "There is at most one amber primary action per screen region. If two things are amber, neither is the call to action.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Word-Beside-Color Rule",
        "body": "Never rely on color alone. Every status pill, violation and approval carries its Arabic word or an icon, because the phone is read in direct sun.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Readable-in-Sun Rule",
        "body": "On a phone nothing is set below 13px, body is 16px, and the figures a person must read at a glance (plate, liters, amount) are visibly larger than everything around them.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Code-Beside-Meaning Rule",
        "body": "A literal code is never shown alone; its Arabic meaning sits next to it.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Isolated Numerals Rule",
        "body": "Numbers use Western numerals with tabular figures, wrapped as left-to-right isolated runs inside the right-to-left flow.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Float-Don't-Draw Rule",
        "body": "Separate regions with radius, shadow and background change. Do not stack divider lines to make structure.",
        "section": "elevation"
      }
    ],
    "dos": [
      "**Do** keep every hue to its meaning: teal for structure and brand, amber for the next action, green for approved, red for broken rules and irreversible actions.",
      "**Do** pair every status with a word or an icon, never color alone.",
      "**Do** keep one amber primary action per screen region.",
      "**Do** use Cairo for all text and JetBrains Mono only for literal codes, plates, timestamps and big numbers.",
      "**Do** put a code next to its Arabic meaning, and wrap numerals as left-to-right isolated runs.",
